{"claims":[{"excerpt":"The Acid Rain Program didn’t use one emissions mechanism for both sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides.","claim":"The program used different emissions mechanisms for sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The sulfur dioxide mechanism applies an aggregate cap and allows trading; one allowance covers one ton. The nitrogen oxides component was rate-based for certain coal-fired units, began in 1996 and did not introduce a cap or allowance trading.","narrative":"The Acid Rain Program addressed sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector, but it did not use one shared emissions mechanism.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source describes distinct mechanisms for the two pollutants.","materiality":"Material distinction, accurately stated."},{"excerpt":"Under Title IV, the sulfur dioxide system used an aggregate cap and tradable allowances.","claim":"Under Title IV, the sulfur dioxide system used an aggregate cap and tradable allowances.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"EPA’s Acid Rain Program addresses sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector under Title IV. The sulfur dioxide mechanism applies an aggregate cap and allows trading;","narrative":"explain that an allowance in its sulfur dioxide system covers one ton under an aggregate cap.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source explicitly identifies Title IV, an aggregate cap, and trading for sulfur dioxide.","materiality":"Material description of the sulfur dioxide mechanism, accurately stated."},{"excerpt":"Each allowance represented permission to emit one ton within that permitted total.","claim":"Each sulfur dioxide allowance represented permission to emit one ton under the aggregate cap.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The sulfur dioxide mechanism applies an aggregate cap and allows trading; one allowance covers one ton.","narrative":"An allowance provides an entry point to the sulfur dioxide system: each covered allowance represents permission for one ton under an aggregate cap with trading.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source states that one allowance covers one ton; the cap is an aggregate permitted total, not actual emissions.","materiality":"Material explanation of an allowance, accurately stated."},{"excerpt":"That system expanded in phases.","claim":"The sulfur dioxide system expanded in phases.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Its phase I began in 1995 with 263 initial units at 110 plants; substitution or compensating units brought the phase I total to 445 units. Phase II began in 2000.","narrative":"The program’s sulfur dioxide coverage expanded across phases","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source describes Phase I and Phase II; the statement does not confuse unit counts with plant counts.","materiality":"Accurately summarizes the chronology."},{"excerpt":"Phase I began in 1995 with 263 units at 110 plants.","claim":"Phase I began in 1995 with 263 units at 110 plants.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Its phase I began in 1995 with 263 initial units at 110 plants;","narrative":"Phase I began in 1995 with 263 initial units at 110 plants;","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The year and both counts match the source; units and plants are distinguished.","materiality":"Material coverage figures, accurately stated."},{"excerpt":"Substitution or compensating units brought the Phase I total to 445 units.","claim":"Substitution or compensating units brought the Phase I total to 445 units.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"substitution or compensating units brought the phase I total to 445 units.","narrative":"substitution or compensating units brought the Phase I total to 445 units.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The total and its relationship to substitution or compensating units match the source.","materiality":"Material coverage figure, accurately stated."},{"excerpt":"Phase II began in 2000,","claim":"Phase II began in 2000.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Phase II began in 2000.","narrative":"Phase II began in 2000,","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The date matches the source.","materiality":"Accurately stated chronology."},{"excerpt":"the final cap in 2010 was 8.95 million tons.","claim":"The final cap in 2010 was 8.95 million tons.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The final 2010 cap was 8.95 million tons.","narrative":"the final cap in 2010 was 8.95 million tons.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The year, quantity, and characterization as a cap match the source; it is not presented as actual emissions.","materiality":"Material cap figure, accurately stated."},{"excerpt":"Nitrogen oxides regulation for certain coal-fired units followed a different track.","claim":"Nitrogen oxides regulation covered certain coal-fired units and followed a different track from the sulfur dioxide system.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The nitrogen oxides component was rate-based for certain coal-fired units, began in 1996 and did not introduce a cap or allowance trading.","narrative":"Contrast that track with nitrogen oxides regulation for certain coal-fired units:","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source limits this description to certain coal-fired units and distinguishes the nitrogen oxides component from sulfur dioxide.","materiality":"Material scope and mechanism distinction, accurately stated."},{"excerpt":"It began in 1996 and was rate-based, with no cap or allowance trading.","claim":"Nitrogen oxides regulation for the specified units began in 1996, was rate-based, and had no cap or allowance trading.","category":"technical","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The nitrogen oxides component was rate-based for certain coal-fired units, began in 1996 and did not introduce a cap or allowance trading.","narrative":"it began in 1996 and was rate-based, without a cap or allowance trading.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The mechanism and start date match the source, and the preceding sentence correctly limits the scope to certain coal-fired units.","materiality":"Material mechanism and chronology, accurately stated."},{"excerpt":"The program addressed both pollutants, but through distinct mechanisms and timelines.","claim":"The program addressed both pollutants, and their mechanisms and timelines were distinct.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"EPA’s Acid Rain Program addresses sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides from the power sector under Title IV. The sulfur dioxide mechanism applies an aggregate cap and allows trading; one allowance covers one ton. Its phase I began in 1995 with 263 initial units at 110 plants; substitution or compensating units brought the phase I total to 445 units. Phase II began in 2000. The final 2010 cap was 8.95 million tons. The nitrogen oxides component was rate-based for certain coal-fired units, began in 1996 and did not introduce a cap or allowance trading.","narrative":"The program’s two pollutants therefore had distinct mechanisms and timelines.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source covers both pollutants and gives different mechanisms and start dates for their respective tracks.","materiality":"Material summary of the program’s scope and structure, accurately stated."}]}

